WEBVTT
Kind: captions
Language: en

00:00:00.550 --> 00:00:05.940
[Music]

00:00:05.940 --> 00:00:09.430
I’d like to show you an abyss.

00:00:09.430 --> 00:00:14.340
Locked within its depths are bewildering and
uncanny abominations.

00:00:14.340 --> 00:00:17.900
The keepers of this abyss believe they are
in control…

00:00:17.900 --> 00:00:20.199
They are wrong.

00:00:20.199 --> 00:00:26.570
This abyss is... the universe of SCP, which
began as a seemingly straightforward website

00:00:26.570 --> 00:00:30.920
where community-members create and classify
abominations.

00:00:30.920 --> 00:00:36.969
Yet from the site, the franchise has grown
so vast, influenced so many people, that trying

00:00:36.969 --> 00:00:42.570
to fathom its totality feels like gazing into
an unending expanse.

00:00:42.570 --> 00:00:48.091
So, for this entry into the archive, I’ll
explore not just the universe itself, but

00:00:48.091 --> 00:00:54.340
how SCP became one of the most gargantuan
and uncontainable forces on the internet.

00:00:54.340 --> 00:00:59.730
And to do that, I have to start at the beginning…

00:00:59.730 --> 00:01:07.220
On June 22nd, 2007, an anonymous user made
a post describing, in dry, academic language,

00:01:07.220 --> 00:01:12.229
a concrete sculpture that moves when left
unobserved — snapping the necks of anyone

00:01:12.229 --> 00:01:14.180
who breaks eye contact with it.

00:01:14.180 --> 00:01:20.750
The sparse description was accompanied by
an image labeled ‘SCP-173’ — at the

00:01:20.750 --> 00:01:23.610
time, meaningless letters and numbers.

00:01:23.610 --> 00:01:28.229
Nobody could have predicted that this was
the birth of something astonishing…

00:01:28.229 --> 00:01:32.820
Flash forwards several months, and numerous
people were creating their own anomalies with

00:01:32.820 --> 00:01:34.909
the same label format.

00:01:34.909 --> 00:01:41.170
Most were rather simple, with SCP-682 for
example, essentially a reptile that’s virtually

00:01:41.170 --> 00:01:42.170
invincible.

00:01:42.170 --> 00:01:47.119
But the universe was starting to take shape,
with the SCP Foundation determined to be a

00:01:47.119 --> 00:01:52.080
secret organization responsible for containing
and studying mysterious phenomena.

00:01:52.080 --> 00:01:55.619
And with this framework, things exploded.

00:01:55.619 --> 00:02:00.920
On the current website, there are more than
seven thousand unique entries, many of which

00:02:00.920 --> 00:02:02.219
are exceedingly original.

00:02:02.219 --> 00:02:10.280
The entry for SCP-2521, for example, contains
no text whatsoever, composed instead of strange

00:02:10.280 --> 00:02:15.120
infographics that are confusing at first,
until you realize they’re telling you about

00:02:15.120 --> 00:02:18.750
a monster that will appear if it’s written
or spoken about.

00:02:18.750 --> 00:02:21.500
It’s some genuinely creative horror.

00:02:21.500 --> 00:02:23.510
But no two entries are the same.

00:02:23.510 --> 00:02:30.190
SCP-106, or The Old Man, is an unfriendly
looking fellow who can corrode any surface

00:02:30.190 --> 00:02:34.020
and hunts humans — classic, effective horror.

00:02:34.020 --> 00:02:39.410
SCP-294 is a coffee machine that can dispense
anything, including abstract concepts like

00:02:39.410 --> 00:02:44.640
musical talent or lethal nuclear waste…
which is definitely a new one.

00:02:44.640 --> 00:02:50.599
Certain posts have achieved unique popularity,
like 049, aka The Plague Doctor — who looks

00:02:50.599 --> 00:02:55.590
like a human in a 15th century plague outfit…
until you look at an x-ray that reveals the

00:02:55.590 --> 00:02:57.640
mask is part of his body.

00:02:57.640 --> 00:03:04.340
This entity claims to have a cure to any disease
— which, spoiler alert, is just death.

00:03:04.340 --> 00:03:10.230
Not every SCP will connect with every person,
but there are so many entries with such distinct

00:03:10.230 --> 00:03:15.260
styles that it’s practically a certainty
that at least one post will draw you in.

00:03:15.260 --> 00:03:19.860
And once you’re in, there’s no getting
out, because the posts themselves are just

00:03:19.860 --> 00:03:21.849
the tip of the iceberg.

00:03:21.849 --> 00:03:26.930
Many of the best entries on the site are text
only, which has inspired a legion of talented

00:03:26.930 --> 00:03:31.959
artists to create their own renderings of
what different SCPs might look like.

00:03:31.959 --> 00:03:38.560
That original SCP 173, for example, no longer
has an image associated with it — because

00:03:38.560 --> 00:03:44.420
the initial photo was actually an unlicensed
picture of the sculpture "Untitled 2004" by

00:03:44.420 --> 00:03:46.590
Japanese artist Izumi Kato.

00:03:46.590 --> 00:03:52.740
But don’t worry, because there are thousands
of images reinterpreting the evil statue — some

00:03:52.740 --> 00:03:57.400
of which are really creative, like this one
from Valdés-Hevia, an artist I’ve covered

00:03:57.400 --> 00:03:58.860
before on the channel.

00:03:58.860 --> 00:04:04.640
But it’s not just famous SCPs that get this
treatment; there are absolutely stunning works

00:04:04.640 --> 00:04:10.110
of art for the most obscure entries — and
I think the reason has to do with the format

00:04:10.110 --> 00:04:12.420
of SCPs themselves.

00:04:12.420 --> 00:04:17.079
When you read an entry that speaks to you,
it’s hard not to obsess over what the anomaly

00:04:17.079 --> 00:04:22.190
might look like, and since many posts have
no art of their own, that obsession often

00:04:22.190 --> 00:04:25.950
explodes out into artistic recreations.

00:04:25.950 --> 00:04:33.520
But when you read an entry like SCP-610 — or
The Flesh That Hates — about a virus that

00:04:33.520 --> 00:04:39.770
turns humans and animals into blobby tendrils
of meat, you don’t just want still images.

00:04:39.770 --> 00:04:41.830
You want to see it move.

00:04:41.830 --> 00:04:43.430
And you can.

00:04:43.430 --> 00:04:49.230
There are literally tens of thousands of animations
and live action short films up on YouTube

00:04:49.230 --> 00:04:54.490
that bring SCPs to life in three glorious,
horrifying dimensions.

00:04:54.490 --> 00:05:00.660
The channel ForlornFoundry is home to some
of my favorites, creating fancy CGI recreations

00:05:00.660 --> 00:05:02.419
of various anomalies.

00:05:02.419 --> 00:05:08.160
But complex visuals effects aren’t the only
way to effectively bring an SCP to life.

00:05:08.160 --> 00:05:14.170
Some of the most chilling ForlornFoundry adaptations
are the simplest ones — like their unbearably

00:05:14.170 --> 00:05:21.490
claustrophobic exploration of SCP-1562: an
infinite tunnel slide that is impossible to

00:05:21.490 --> 00:05:24.889
escape from once you get stuck within its
chasmal depths.

00:05:24.889 --> 00:05:31.990
Or the similar SCP-087, a pitch-black staircase
you can never reach the bottom of.

00:05:31.990 --> 00:05:37.389
My favorite moment in this video is when someone
trapped on the stairs drops a coin off the

00:05:37.389 --> 00:05:43.380
railing, and the anticipated sound of the
coin hitting the ground just… never comes.

00:05:43.380 --> 00:05:49.240
It’s seriously clever filmmaking, conveying
the dread and anxiety of the original entry.

00:05:49.240 --> 00:05:54.009
And I think that’s part of what motivates
ForlornFoundry and similar channels — a

00:05:54.009 --> 00:05:58.819
desire to recreate how the best SCPs feel
when you read them.

00:05:58.819 --> 00:06:02.449
And I’m guessing that’s why there are
so many adaptations of SCP-3008…

00:06:02.449 --> 00:06:03.449
“IKEA.

00:06:03.449 --> 00:06:07.610
A place where children could play while their
parents shop.

00:06:07.610 --> 00:06:11.010
Where friends could share good cheer.

00:06:11.010 --> 00:06:14.410
This vision has come to pass.

00:06:14.410 --> 00:06:21.819
IKEA has just opened its 70th store-” The
most renowned SCP in this sub-category of

00:06:21.819 --> 00:06:28.669
immeasurable spaces is the infinite IKEA — an
extradimensional version of the already labyrinthine

00:06:28.669 --> 00:06:32.759
retail store that literally never ends.

00:06:32.759 --> 00:06:37.889
Something about wandering the furniture displays
in perpetuity, forever unable to find your

00:06:37.889 --> 00:06:42.569
way back to the entrance, has struck a chord
with the internet — leading to countless

00:06:42.569 --> 00:06:43.970
digital recreations.

00:06:43.970 --> 00:06:49.569
I’ve seen references to the infinite IKEA
well beyond the sphere of SCP.

00:06:49.569 --> 00:06:54.949
There are entire fan games about this concept,
letting you get lost among the endless furniture

00:06:54.949 --> 00:06:58.230
displays as a gnawing loneliness sets in.

00:06:58.230 --> 00:07:02.000
It’s a sensation that I find… strangely
familiar.

00:07:02.000 --> 00:07:07.200
I’m sure many of us have early memories
of getting separated from our parents in the

00:07:07.200 --> 00:07:08.460
supermarket.

00:07:08.460 --> 00:07:14.069
Even if it was just for a brief moment, the
aisles suddenly felt larger and more threatening,

00:07:14.069 --> 00:07:17.240
and there was a sense that you’d never find
a way out.

00:07:17.240 --> 00:07:22.879
I think many of the most well-known SCPs are
rooted in these half-remembered childhood

00:07:22.879 --> 00:07:26.360
fears, which helps explain their infectious
popularity.

00:07:26.360 --> 00:07:32.710
There’s something universal about the vertigo
of a really big staircase or, again, an empty

00:07:32.710 --> 00:07:37.860
store that allows SCPs to leave a wide audience
feeling unsettled.

00:07:37.860 --> 00:07:40.789
…But they’re also kind of hilarious.

00:07:40.789 --> 00:07:47.770
Like, being stuck forever in an IKEA is disturbing,
yet at the same time, really funny in a way

00:07:47.770 --> 00:07:49.610
that’s difficult to quantify.

00:07:49.610 --> 00:07:55.349
When night falls in the store, faceless monsters
wearing IKEA employee uniforms start hunting

00:07:55.349 --> 00:07:59.960
you through the dark — and while logically
that should make things scarier, to me it

00:07:59.960 --> 00:08:02.199
only adds to the humor.

00:08:02.199 --> 00:08:07.770
This paradoxical undercurrent of comedy is
present in a lot of SCPs.

00:08:07.770 --> 00:08:14.199
SCP-1609 was a chair that helpfully teleported
to people when they needed to sit down, until

00:08:14.199 --> 00:08:19.380
someone put it through a woodchipper, turning
it into a rageful pile of mulch that teleports

00:08:19.380 --> 00:08:20.710
into peoples’ lungs.

00:08:20.710 --> 00:08:23.440
And while murderous sawdust would be terrifying…

00:08:23.440 --> 00:08:26.199
it’s pretty hysterical in the abstract.

00:08:26.199 --> 00:08:33.289
And this, I believe, is the other massive
component to SCP’s unparalleled expansion.

00:08:33.289 --> 00:08:38.649
Even if every entry won’t scare you, chances
are at least a few will make you laugh.

00:08:38.649 --> 00:08:45.230
And the more of these stories you read, the
funnier it all seems to get…

00:08:45.230 --> 00:08:51.260
The question of why a never-ending ensemble
of terrors is so amusing can best be answered

00:08:51.260 --> 00:08:56.300
by the genre-bending horror-comedy film The
Cabin in the Woods.

00:08:56.300 --> 00:09:01.270
Probably the closest thing to a big-budget
SCP movie, the story involves five friends

00:09:01.270 --> 00:09:05.990
vacationing at a remote cabin unprepared for
the terrors that await them.

00:09:05.990 --> 00:09:11.210
What at first feels like every horror movie
ever quickly goes off the rails, as we learn

00:09:11.210 --> 00:09:16.700
the whole thing is being orchestrated by a
secret organization that keeps every conceivable

00:09:16.700 --> 00:09:21.779
type of monster contained in an underground
facility — monsters they then unleash on

00:09:21.779 --> 00:09:25.260
unsuspecting youths to appease the old gods.

00:09:25.260 --> 00:09:29.100
This overabundance of horrors is the key to
the film’s humor.

00:09:29.100 --> 00:09:34.820
A single monster stalking a group of innocents
is horrifying, but a thousand monsters of

00:09:34.820 --> 00:09:39.340
contrasting origins and genres starts to become,
well, comical.

00:09:39.340 --> 00:09:44.880
And it’s this principle that makes the SCP
series so entertaining.

00:09:44.880 --> 00:09:50.260
Yet beyond the absurdity of both The Cabin
in the Woods and SCP is a stark commentary

00:09:50.260 --> 00:09:52.250
on desensitization.

00:09:52.250 --> 00:09:57.300
In the film, the technicians in charge of
the facility are completely numb to the reality

00:09:57.300 --> 00:10:02.329
of what they’re doing, taking bets on which
monsters will slaughter the cabin-goers.

00:10:02.329 --> 00:10:08.610
In many SCP stories, Foundation employees
behave the same way, having become apathetic

00:10:08.610 --> 00:10:12.800
to human suffering after a lifetime of containing
anomalies.

00:10:12.800 --> 00:10:15.810
And both narratives implicate the viewer as
well.

00:10:15.810 --> 00:10:21.410
In Cabin in the Woods, the Old Gods are a
clear stand in for the audience —unfeeling

00:10:21.410 --> 00:10:23.170
spectators who demand sacrifice.

00:10:23.170 --> 00:10:29.550
Likewise, with SCP, the more entries you read,
the more you inhabit the perspective of a

00:10:29.550 --> 00:10:37.730
Foundation employee clocking in to their job,
increasingly unafraid of the nightmares within...

00:10:37.730 --> 00:10:42.700
The ability of SCP to immerse you, to make
you feel like you’re a part of the universe

00:10:42.700 --> 00:10:46.829
— has made its stranglehold on the internet
inescapable.

00:10:46.829 --> 00:10:52.139
It leaves you with a desire to go deeper,
to experience the narratives firsthand — and

00:10:52.139 --> 00:10:57.850
I’d wager that’s why games based on the
franchise are so insanely prevalent.

00:10:57.850 --> 00:11:03.660
SCP: Containment Breach is quite possibly
the most well-known thing to ever crawl out

00:11:03.660 --> 00:11:05.089
of the franchise.

00:11:05.089 --> 00:11:10.380
The indie game has an easy to understand haunted
house format, where the deadliest anomalies

00:11:10.380 --> 00:11:13.790
escape and hunt you through a dimly lit facility.

00:11:13.790 --> 00:11:18.942
And shortly after its release, Containment
Breach exploded on YouTube, with the view

00:11:18.942 --> 00:11:24.110
counts on playthroughs so high, I’d wager
it’s how many were introduced to SCP in

00:11:24.110 --> 00:11:25.180
the first place.

00:11:25.180 --> 00:11:31.279
But like the wiki itself, SCP games have come
a long way since Containment Breach — with

00:11:31.279 --> 00:11:36.810
the most impressive from a standpoint of sheer
creativity probably being Secret Files.

00:11:36.810 --> 00:11:42.570
Released in 2022, this title puts you in the
shoes of a newcomer to the Foundation who

00:11:42.570 --> 00:11:46.450
learns about past SCPs while working in the
archives.

00:11:46.450 --> 00:11:52.450
The story is divided into chapters, each a
gameplay and art-style so distinct, they might

00:11:52.450 --> 00:11:54.410
as well be different games.

00:11:54.410 --> 00:12:01.760
For example, one chapter is dedicated to the
famous SCP-701, or The Hanged King's Tragedy

00:12:01.760 --> 00:12:06.639
— a 17th century play that causes anyone
who performs it to lose their mind.

00:12:06.639 --> 00:12:12.660
Appropriately, the chapter itself is chaotic
and psychedelic, with the player never certain

00:12:12.660 --> 00:12:16.690
what’s real and what’s a product of the
anomaly affecting their mind.

00:12:16.690 --> 00:12:22.620
From an artistic perspective, it’s one of
the more immersive ways to experience a reality-bending

00:12:22.620 --> 00:12:26.600
SCP, and can at times be genuinely spooky.

00:12:26.600 --> 00:12:33.100
On the opposite end of the tonal spectrum
is the chapter on SCP-1762 — a cardboard

00:12:33.100 --> 00:12:39.080
box that acts as a portal to a Narnia-esque
magical land that paper dragons come out of.

00:12:39.080 --> 00:12:44.720
The original entry is famous for its uniquely
non-horror approach, and to reflect this the

00:12:44.720 --> 00:12:47.810
art style of this chapter is bright and painterly.

00:12:47.810 --> 00:12:53.089
And the gameplay is equally charming — instead
of running for your life, you use stickers

00:12:53.089 --> 00:12:58.230
to bandage the wings of paper dragons and
practice your penny flute for the big recital.

00:12:58.230 --> 00:13:03.170
The whole chapter, I kept waiting for things
to get dark, and while like the original entry

00:13:03.170 --> 00:13:08.200
there is some truly tear-jerking stuff as
the magical land starts to fade away — for

00:13:08.200 --> 00:13:12.880
the most part, the chapter retains its storybook
presentation throughout.

00:13:12.880 --> 00:13:18.650
Taken as a whole, Secret Files is the ultimate
proof of SCP’s versatility — that it can

00:13:18.650 --> 00:13:23.910
be used to tell stories in any genre.

00:13:23.910 --> 00:13:29.570
The infinite variability of SCP means that
no single game could ever embody the series

00:13:29.570 --> 00:13:33.130
in its totality — which hasn’t stopped
fans from trying.

00:13:33.130 --> 00:13:39.350
SCP: Fragmented Minds is an upcoming crowdsourced
game that seems massively ambitious, taking

00:13:39.350 --> 00:13:44.410
a more combat-oriented approach in contrast
to the more passive Secret Files.

00:13:44.410 --> 00:13:50.010
Fragmented Minds seems to have a lot of passion
behind it, but more than that, it seems symptomatic

00:13:50.010 --> 00:13:51.470
of a larger dream.

00:13:51.470 --> 00:13:58.540
There’s a phantom of a big-budget, triple-A
SCP game that haunts the ever-growing fanbase,

00:13:58.540 --> 00:14:03.760
the feverish hope that one day, a mainline
studio might take a swing at this franchise.

00:14:03.760 --> 00:14:09.389
It doesn’t seem impossible — in fact,
it might have already happened.

00:14:09.389 --> 00:14:14.949
The majority of the game Control revolves
around the secretive Federal Bureau of Control,

00:14:14.949 --> 00:14:20.550
an off-the-books government agency created
to seek out, contain, and categorize supernatural

00:14:20.550 --> 00:14:22.380
items called Objects of Power.

00:14:22.380 --> 00:14:30.330
And if that sounds familiar, it should, because
Control and SCP are explicitly, irrevocably,

00:14:30.330 --> 00:14:31.330
intertwined.

00:14:31.330 --> 00:14:36.100
The makers of Control have stated that the
game’s story and atmosphere are heavily

00:14:36.100 --> 00:14:38.779
influenced by the SCP series.

00:14:38.779 --> 00:14:44.410
Think about that for a second: a major, award-winning
company made what is, on some level, a fan

00:14:44.410 --> 00:14:49.420
game of an indie-internet horror anthology
— that’s pretty unheard of.

00:14:49.420 --> 00:14:52.970
But the connection between the franchises
goes deeper.

00:14:52.970 --> 00:14:58.240
Control takes place within an altered building
called The Oldest House — an ever-changing

00:14:58.240 --> 00:15:01.590
structure where the Bureau has set up headquarters.

00:15:01.590 --> 00:15:06.680
Nothing about The Oldest House makes scientific
sense — its interior is infinitely larger

00:15:06.680 --> 00:15:12.139
than its exterior, certain rooms are thresholds
to other dimensions, and the building itself

00:15:12.139 --> 00:15:16.070
can only be seen by someone explicitly looking
for it.

00:15:16.070 --> 00:15:20.890
And yet despite this, every description of
The Oldest House and the altered objects housed

00:15:20.890 --> 00:15:26.380
within are written in the same dry, confident
tone — deeply reminiscent of the language

00:15:26.380 --> 00:15:28.620
used in SCP entries.

00:15:28.620 --> 00:15:33.699
The same confidence extends to in-game recordings
you can find, where researchers will discuss

00:15:33.699 --> 00:15:37.339
mind-shattering phenomena like it’s all
business as usual.

00:15:37.339 --> 00:15:42.399
There’s even an insane puppet show called
Threshold Kids the Bureau created to reassure

00:15:42.399 --> 00:15:45.459
children that there’s definitely nothing
to worry about.

00:15:45.459 --> 00:15:50.230
Sure, the building seems malignant and is
full of floating zombies, but everything is

00:15:50.230 --> 00:15:51.950
under control.

00:15:51.950 --> 00:15:57.350
It only takes a few minutes of actual gameplay
to make it clear that no one in the game Control

00:15:57.350 --> 00:16:00.519
is actually, well, in control.

00:16:00.519 --> 00:16:05.850
The notion that layers of bureaucracy and
paperwork can actually contain the unfathomable

00:16:05.850 --> 00:16:08.610
horrors long term is just an illusion.

00:16:08.610 --> 00:16:12.529
And the same is true at the SCP Foundation.

00:16:12.529 --> 00:16:17.199
Despite the Foundation’s best efforts, the
deeper into the lore you get, the more patterns

00:16:17.199 --> 00:16:20.020
of administrative dysfunction emerge.

00:16:20.020 --> 00:16:25.690
As in Control, The Foundation is ultimately
an underequipped group fighting a losing battle

00:16:25.690 --> 00:16:30.980
against forces they can’t truly understand.

00:16:30.980 --> 00:16:36.670
Many of the most complex recent stories set
in the SCP universe emphasize this hopelessness

00:16:36.670 --> 00:16:41.399
— this sense that the Foundation isn’t
as in control as they’d like to believe.

00:16:41.399 --> 00:16:48.440
The recent 20-minute film 096 by Klay Abele
explores the tragedy and horror that occur

00:16:48.440 --> 00:16:52.040
when the cracks in the Foundation’s system
start to show.

00:16:52.040 --> 00:16:58.070
The film’s title comes from SCP-096 — a
monster that hunts down anyone who looks at

00:16:58.070 --> 00:17:03.310
its face — whether it be directly, via video
recording, or even a photograph.

00:17:03.310 --> 00:17:09.449
Yet the other monster haunting the film is
the Foundation itself — a faceless bureaucracy

00:17:09.449 --> 00:17:15.480
laden with internal corruption that can be
dangerous as the anomalies it contains.

00:17:15.480 --> 00:17:20.040
The fact that this franchise went from a bunch
of people sharing spooky ideas on a message

00:17:20.040 --> 00:17:23.910
board to something this thematically dense
is pretty astonishing.

00:17:23.910 --> 00:17:30.850
And 096 isn’t the only SCP adaptation of
its level of quality on YouTube.

00:17:30.850 --> 00:17:36.950
SCP: Overlord by Steven Handcock and Evan
Muir is an even lengthier short film that

00:17:36.950 --> 00:17:40.039
captures a similar atmosphere of powerlessness.

00:17:40.039 --> 00:17:44.960
A tightly-wound thriller, it follows a group
of Foundation military operatives who are

00:17:44.960 --> 00:17:50.620
slowly picked off by an incomprehensible threat
— made all the more unsettling by the fact

00:17:50.620 --> 00:17:54.800
that the film never tells you which SCP is
taking them out.

00:17:54.800 --> 00:17:59.510
These filmmakers have made other shorts set
in this universe that also don’t fully explain

00:17:59.510 --> 00:18:03.800
which anomaly the protagonists are dealing
with — which helps put the viewer in their

00:18:03.800 --> 00:18:06.090
shoes and makes everything tenser.

00:18:06.090 --> 00:18:12.460
There’s a clear hunger for cinematic takes
on this franchise — SCP: Overlord, for example,

00:18:12.460 --> 00:18:15.150
has almost eighteen million views.

00:18:15.150 --> 00:18:20.480
As was the case with the games, I’m sure
a lot of fans discovered SCP through these

00:18:20.480 --> 00:18:21.480
videos.

00:18:21.480 --> 00:18:25.980
And some of these new fans will inevitably
create their own SCPs, which will in turn

00:18:25.980 --> 00:18:31.929
produce more fan content which will reach
new people, continuing an infinite, self-perpetuating

00:18:31.929 --> 00:18:39.150
cycle — The idea of a website dedicated
to uncanny terrors that slowly takes over

00:18:39.150 --> 00:18:44.640
the internet is exactly the sort of thing
one might expect to find on the SCP wiki — but

00:18:44.640 --> 00:18:49.110
in the ultimate twist, that description applies
to the wiki itself.

00:18:49.110 --> 00:18:54.920
The SCP series and its popularity is the kind
of anomalous phenomena the Foundation would

00:18:54.920 --> 00:18:56.270
want to contain.

00:18:56.270 --> 00:18:58.520
But it can’t be contained.

00:19:06.658 --> 00:19:11.748
There are graphic novels, short stories, other
organizations, alternate universes — the

00:19:11.748 --> 00:19:18.158
fandom seems to be ever-expanding, completely
out of anyone’s control…

00:19:18.158 --> 00:19:20.128
And perhaps that’s as it should be.

00:19:20.128 --> 00:19:25.498
While massive and unwieldy, there is such
a range of styles and genres across this franchise

00:19:25.498 --> 00:19:28.468
that I truly believe it has something for
everyone.

00:19:28.468 --> 00:19:34.078
In the end, the most important thing the SCP
Foundation offers is a canvas, upon which

00:19:34.078 --> 00:19:37.018
ordinary, passionate creators can leave their
mark.

00:19:37.018 --> 00:19:41.958
I think that’s the real secret to its success
and why this abyss — unfathomable though

00:19:41.958 --> 00:19:45.968
it may be — is worth staring into.

00:19:45.968 --> 00:19:48.358
As always, thanks for watching.

00:19:48.358 --> 00:19:53.108
If you enjoyed this entry, please lend your
support by liking, subscribing, and hitting

00:19:53.108 --> 00:19:57.638
the notification icon to stay up to date on
all things Curious.

00:19:57.638 --> 00:20:06.998
See you in the next video.

